    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically just a balance of chemicals? i've noticed sometimes my starter just looks... messy or weirdly active for no reason. is there anything else in there besides those two that could be messing with the chemistry or the smell?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, it is fundamentally a chemical balance, but it's far more complex than just two actors! You are absolutely right to notice the \"messy\" phases; those are often signs of a microbial shift, like a sudden surge of wild, less desirable *Lactobacillus* strains, or perhaps a temporary dominance by mold spores if the conditions aren't quite right. A healthy starter is a carefully managed ecosystem, not just a chemical reaction, and sometimes the wild, uninvited guests—like mold or too many opportunistic bacteria—can throw the whole delicate balance into chaos.\n\nIf your starter is smelling funky or looking strange, it's usually because something else has taken the reins, which is often related to temperature, hydration, or the flour you're using. Sometimes, other yeasts or bacteria present in the flour itself—the wild flora—can start to proliferate if the dominant strains are stressed. These unwanted microbes can produce off-flavors or different byproducts that disrupt the clean, predictable chemistry you are aiming for.\n\nThink of it like a tiny, volatile swamp; if you don't manage the water levels (hydration) or the feeding schedule (food supply), some aggressive, smelly little creatures will move in and",
      "source": "target",
